# WEB-AUDIT-139 / 2026-05-10 P2-GROUPED-CLOSER updates:
#   1. Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy upgraded from `same-origin` to
#      `same-origin-allow-popups` so window.open() to Calendly + Stripe
#      Checkout retains a usable opener handle (required for the popup to
#      message back to the marketing site after booking/payment).
#   2. Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy switched from `unsafe-none` to
#      `credentialless` (NOT `require-corp`). Rationale: `require-corp`
#      breaks the Calendly iframe — Calendly's CDN (calendly.com,
#      *.calendly.com) does not serve `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy`
#      headers, so the iframe is blocked entirely under require-corp.
#      `credentialless` retains COEP isolation benefits (cross-origin
#      isolation enables SharedArrayBuffer + high-resolution timers)
#      while loading cross-origin sub-resources without credentials —
#      Calendly's iframe loads as anonymous which is fine because the
#      embed is read-only.
#   3. Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy upgraded from `same-origin` to
#      `same-site` so subdomain embeds (app.crowagent.ai → crowagent.ai
#      og-image, crowagent.ai → app.crowagent.ai assets) load without a
#      CORP block. Calendly's own resources are served from third-party
#      origin and are governed by CSP frame-src + COEP credentialless,
#      not by our CORP header.
#   4. Surrogate-Control: max-age=14400 — Cloudflare Pages honours this
#      as the edge-cache TTL hint. Aligns with the 4h CDN TTL noted in
#      §18 of CLAUDE.md (production smoke methodology). Origin-only
#      header; the Cloudflare proxy strips it before the response
#      reaches the browser (per RFC 3040).
#   5. CSP `manifest-src 'self'` added explicitly. The marketing site
#      ships `/manifest.json` for the PWA shim; without an explicit
#      `manifest-src` directive Chrome falls back to default-src which
#      is `'self'` so the rule was already satisfied transitively, but
#      Firefox enforces the explicit directive — adding it removes the
#      Firefox console warning.
#   6. CSP `media-src 'self' https://eu.i.posthog.com` added. PostHog
#      session recordings (Phase 2 feature) embed short audio/video
#      snippets via media element on the EU ingest endpoint. Adding
#      this here pre-empts the future enablement (no-op today since
#      session recording is currently disabled in production but enabled
#      in PostHog dashboard).
#   7. CSP `frame-src` expanded from `https://calendly.com
#      https://challenges.cloudflare.com` to additionally allow
#      `https://*.calendly.com`. Calendly serves event pages on
#      sub-domains (calendly.com/your-org/30min loads chrome from
#      static.calendly.com) and the wildcard sub-domain rule is
#      required so the iframe doesn't redirect-block on the
#      sub-domain hop. `'self'` added so SVG mockups can be embedded
#      via <iframe srcdoc> if needed (defensive; currently unused).
#   8. Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: SKIPPED. The Cloudflare
#      Pages `_headers` file does not expose a way to scope a header
#      to a preview-only origin (the same `_headers` ships to both
#      production and preview). To enable a Report-Only CSP on a
#      staging origin would require either a Cloudflare Pages function
#      or a separate _headers file in a `dev/` subdir — both out of
#      scope for the current marketing-site stack. Documented here so
#      a future operator knows it was considered and consciously
#      deferred. The enforced CSP itself already report-uri's to the
#      platform endpoint so violations ARE captured site-wide.
  # FINAL-2-LCP 2026-05-10:
  # - style-src adds 'unsafe-inline' so the critical-above-fold <style>
  #   block (Assets/css/critical-above-fold.css inlined into <head> on
  #   the 7 LCP-target pages: index + 6 product landings) can paint the
  #   H1 LCP element BEFORE the full styles.min.css (~187KB) finishes
  #   downloading. The inline-styles ratchet (verify-no-inline-styles.js)
  #   still enforces 0 `style="..."` attributes; this concession only
  #   covers <style> blocks in <head>.
  # - script-src-attr upgraded from 'none' to 'unsafe-hashes' + a single
  #   sha256 hash for the deferred-stylesheet onload="this.rel='stylesheet'"
  #   handler. This is a much tighter relaxation than 'unsafe-inline':
  #   only the exact handler whose source text hashes to this value is
  #   allowed; any other inline event handler is still blocked. The
  #   handler is necessary because we cannot ship a new external JS
  #   module under the FINAL-2-LCP constraints (only service-worker.js
  #   is editable in the JS surface).
# [R2.5.1 MKT-2 SECURITY F-2/F-3] ROOT-CAUSE FIX: the global security-header
# block below previously had NO path pattern, so Cloudflare Pages treated it as
# invalid and applied NONE of these headers to any route. Verified live: the
# pages.dev origin emitted only the Pages defaults (Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*,
# nosniff, Referrer-Policy) and NO Content-Security-Policy / Strict-Transport-
# Security / X-Frame-Options / Permissions-Policy. Adding the required `/*` path
# makes the whole block apply to EVERY request (clean URLs like /, /pricing
# included, which never matched the /*.html rule further down).
# `! Access-Control-Allow-Origin` detaches the Cloudflare Pages default ACAO:*
# (F-3) — a static public marketing site needs no CORS wildcard.
# script-src / script-src-attr allow 'unsafe-inline' because this is static
# Cloudflare hosting (no server → no per-request nonce) and the pages ship inline
# bootstrap scripts + deferred-stylesheet onload handlers; a hash/nonce-based
# tightening is a follow-up that requires a build step to inject the digests.
/*
  ! Access-Control-Allow-Origin
  # 2026-07-30: removed https://fonts.googleapis.com from style-src and
  # https://fonts.gstatic.com from font-src. Fonts were self-hosted on 2026-07-29
  # specifically so the site stops handing Google the visitor's IP on every page load,
  # but the CSP still permitted both origins, so an accidental reference would have
  # silently worked and undone that. Verified unused three ways before removing: the
  # only mentions left in fonts-selfhosted.css are comments (lines 3 and 111),
  # stripe-sample/ is not in the dist allowlist and does not ship, and a live run
  # across index, crowmark, pricing, contact, blog/index and security made ZERO
  # requests to either origin.
  # Calendly, Turnstile and PostHog entries were checked at the same time and KEPT —
  # all three are still referenced by served pages.
  #
  # ── CROWAGENT-WEB-N, 2026-08-12: CLOUDFLARE WEB ANALYTICS WAS BLOCKED BY THIS
  #    VERY LINE FOR AT LEAST 74 DAYS ─────────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # Sentry issue CROWAGENT-WEB-N, 129 occurrences, first 2026-05-29, last
  # 2026-08-10, all `disposition: enforce`:
  #
  #   blockedUri         https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js/v4513…
  #   effectiveDirective script-src-elem
  #   documentUri        https://crowagent.ai/blog/
  #
  # WHY NO GATE SAW IT, AND WHY NO GATE COULD. The beacon is not in this
  # repository and is not in `astro/dist`; Cloudflare injects it at the edge,
  # into the HTML response, after every build step has finished. Verified both
  # ways on 2026-08-12: `git grep cloudflareinsights` finds nothing in the tree,
  # and the built `blog/index.html` contains exactly one `<script src>`, the
  # Astro module bundle. check-csp.js walks `dist` and checks the origins it
  # FINDS, so an origin that only ever appears in the browser is outside the
  # only corpus it can read. That is the defect class, not an oversight in it:
  # a build-derived gate cannot assert a requirement the build does not state.
  # check-csp-required-origins.js exists to state such requirements explicitly.
  #
  # `script-src-elem` IS NOT DECLARED AND MUST NOT BE. The browser reported it
  # as the effective directive because it falls back to `script-src` when
  # absent, so naming the origin in `script-src` below is what unblocks the
  # beacon. Declaring a separate `script-src-elem` would be actively worse: it
  # governs inline <script> elements too, so it would have to carry a duplicate
  # of the 16 SHA-256 hashes that copy-cf-config.js computes from `dist` on
  # every build, and the copy would go stale the first time it was missed.
  #
  # CONNECT-SRC IS REQUIRED TOO, AND THE ABSENCE OF A REPORT DOES NOT SAY
  # OTHERWISE. Verified by reading the beacon itself on 2026-08-12, not from
  # documentation: `sendObjectBeacon()` sends to `i || "/cdn-cgi/rum?" + t`,
  # and its caller sets that override to "https://cloudflareinsights.com/cdn-cgi/rum"
  # when the injected `data-cf-beacon` payload carries no `version` key, or to
  # null (same-origin, covered by 'self') when it does. Which of the two shapes
  # Cloudflare injects here cannot be read from this repository, so the apex
  # host is allowed to cover both. Note that `connect-src` violations could
  # never have appeared in Sentry alongside the ones above: the script was
  # blocked before it ran, so it never reached a send. A clean report on a
  # directive downstream of a blocked script is not evidence about that
  # directive.
  #
  # TWO HOSTS, NEITHER A WILDCARD: `static.cloudflareinsights.com` serves the
  # beacon (script-src) and the apex `cloudflareinsights.com` receives the
  # measurements (connect-src). They are different hosts and one does not imply
  # the other. Nothing else is relaxed here: no 'unsafe-inline', no
  # 'unsafe-eval', no `*`.
  #
  # IF THE OWNER DOES NOT WANT CLOUDFLARE WEB ANALYTICS, the correct fix is the
  # opposite of this one: turn the injection off in the Cloudflare dashboard
  # (Web Analytics, or Pages project settings) and delete both hosts from this
  # line and from the required-origins gate. Allowing it is the choice made
  # here because it restores a capability the account is already carrying, and
  # the beacon is cookieless and stores nothing, so it does not contradict the
  # cookie policy's statement that this website stores nothing.
  #
  # ── R27-SENTRY-04, measured live 2026-08-17: A SECOND EDGE INJECTION, AND
  #    THIS ONE CANNOT BE FIXED BY NAMING AN ORIGIN ───────────────────────────
  #
  # Sentry CROWAGENT-WEB-N also carries 26 events whose documentUri is
  # https://crowagent.ai/ rather than the app. The blocked script is Cloudflare
  # JavaScript Detections, and it is INLINE:
  #
  #   window.__CF$cv$params={r:'a2cca2533cf6fce5',t:'MTc4NzAxMTE4Mg=='};
  #   var a=document.createElement('script');
  #   a.src='/cdn-cgi/challenge-platform/scripts/jsd/main.js';
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE LIVE RESPONSE, not derived from this tree. Present on 6 of 6
  # HTML pages fetched (/, /pricing/, /contact/, /faq/, /blog/, /accessibility/)
  # and on the 404. It is absent from `astro/dist`, so it is injected at the
  # edge after every build step, exactly like the beacon above.
  #
  # A HASH CANNOT COVER IT, AND THAT IS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN OPINION. The `r:`
  # value is per request: three consecutive fetches of / returned r values
  # a2cca30a5e11054f, a2cca30b6a29d1f9 and a2cca30c8baa774f. The script body
  # therefore has a different SHA-256 on every single response, so the 16-hash
  # allowlist that copy-cf-config.js computes from `dist` can never contain it.
  # This is a permanent enforce-mode violation by construction.
  #
  # A NONCE IS THE VENDOR'S ANSWER AND IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE. Cloudflare
  # documents that it parses the CSP response header and copies a script-src
  # nonce onto the scripts it injects. A nonce must differ per response; this
  # file is a static header table served by Cloudflare Pages, so every response
  # would carry the same value and a fixed nonce is a fixed password. It would
  # also have to be stamped onto the site's own 16 inline scripts, which are
  # emitted by a static build. Same conclusion recorded in copy-cf-config.js.
  #
  # "TURN IT OFF IN THE DASHBOARD" IS NOT A TOGGLE. Cloudflare's own
  # documentation states that for Bot Fight Mode customers JavaScript Detections
  # is automatically enabled AND CANNOT BE DISABLED. So the dashboard route is
  # to turn Bot Fight Mode off for the zone, which is a bot-posture decision and
  # not a CSP fix.
  #
  # THE ONE REMEDY THAT LIVES IN THIS FILE, and why it is not taken unilaterally:
  # Cloudflare does not inject JavaScript Detections when the origin response
  # carries `Cache-Control: no-transform`. That would end the violation at
  # source, with no change to the policy at all. It also suppresses the
  # cf.bot_management.js_detection.passed signal and every other Cloudflare HTML
  # transform on this zone, so it trades a telemetry-only violation for a
  # reduction in bot detection. That is an owner decision, recorded in
  # OWNER-ACTIONS.md, not one to take inside a headers file.
  #
  # DO NOT "FIX" THIS BY WEAKENING script-src. Re-adding 'unsafe-inline' would
  # silence the report and hand every injected inline script permission to run,
  # which is the entire threat this policy exists to stop. That route is closed
  # by a gate rather than by this comment: check-csp-required-origins.js fails
  # the deploy chain on a blanket source in script-src. Red-proved 2026-08-17,
  # planting 'unsafe-inline' back into a copy of dist/_headers took it from
  # exit 0 to exit 1 reporting "script-src carries 'unsafe-inline'".
  #
  # NO USER-FACING LOSS. This is bot telemetry, not site behaviour, so the page
  # works identically with the script blocked. That is why it is a P3.
  #
  # A CORRECTION TO THE BLOCK ABOVE, measured the same day: the Web Analytics
  # beacon is NOT being injected any more. Zero occurrences of `beacon.min.js`,
  # `data-cf-beacon` or `cloudflareinsights` across the same 6 live pages. The
  # violations stopped because the beacon stopped arriving, NOT because this
  # policy started permitting it, so allowing the two hosts has not restored
  # analytics. They stay named here because the permission must already be in
  # place on the day injection is switched back on; the gate asserts they are
  # permitted, which is still true and still what is wanted.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: https:; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://crowagent.ai https://assets.calendly.com https://eu.posthog.com https://eu.i.posthog.com https://eu-assets.i.posthog.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; script-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'; connect-src 'self' https://crowagent.ai https://app.crowagent.ai https://crowagent-platform-production.up.railway.app https://eu.posthog.com https://eu.i.posthog.com https://eu-assets.i.posthog.com https://cloudflareinsights.com; frame-src 'self' https://calendly.com https://*.calendly.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self' https://app.crowagent.ai; object-src 'none'; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; media-src 'self' https://eu.i.posthog.com; upgrade-insecure-requests; report-to default; report-uri https://crowagent.report-uri.com/r/d/csp/enforce
  Reporting-Endpoints: default="https://app.crowagent.ai/api/csp-report"
  Report-To: {"group":"default","max_age":10886400,"endpoints":[{"url":"https://app.crowagent.ai/api/csp-report"}]}
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # S-8 hardening 2026-05-21: expanded long-tail Permissions-Policy denials.
  # Every modern browser API surface that the marketing site does NOT use is
  # explicitly opted-out. Reduces attack surface from compromised third-party.
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), bluetooth=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), fullscreen=(self), gamepad=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), hid=(), idle-detection=(), interest-cohort=(), keyboard-map=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), picture-in-picture=(self "https://assets.calendly.com"), publickey-credentials-get=(), screen-wake-lock=(), serial=(), sync-xhr=(), usb=(), web-share=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-site
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless
  # Default to a SHORT edge cache so HTML pages — including CLEAN URLs
  # (/, /pricing, /about, /blog/*, /tools/*) which never match the /*.html
  # rule below — revalidate quickly. Previously this was max-age=14400, so
  # every clean-URL page was edge+browser cached for 4 HOURS and site changes
  # took up to 4h to appear (2026-06-03 fix). Versioned/immutable assets
  # restore their 1-year edge cache via their own rules further down.
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, s-maxage=300, stale-while-revalidate=60
  Surrogate-Control: max-age=300
  # 2026-08-01: the 2026-06-03 fix above cut the TTL but set the wrong header,
  # so it only ever half-worked. MEASURED after shipping a new homepage: `/`,
  # `/crowmark` and `/pricing` all returned `cf-cache-status: HIT` with an
  # IDENTICAL `Age: 5261` — 87 minutes on an `s-maxage=300`. The owner could
  # not see the new homepage at all while the origin was serving it correctly.
  #
  # Cloudflare obeys CDN-Cache-Control over Cache-Control for its own edge, and
  # only `/*.html` below carried it. Clean URLs never match `/*.html`, which is
  # exactly the gap the comment above describes but did not close. A Pages
  # deployment does NOT purge those entries, so a content deploy could sit
  # invisible for an hour or more with no way to tell from the build logs.
  #
  # HTML is now never edge-cached. Every asset path below re-asserts its own
  # CDN-Cache-Control so nothing loses its long edge TTL — check those rules
  # before changing this one.
  CDN-Cache-Control: no-store

# --- HTML pages: short cache, revalidate on edge ---
/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, s-maxage=300, stale-while-revalidate=60
  CDN-Cache-Control: no-store

# --- [R2.2 F-042] JS modules + page CSS: keep the CDN EDGE warm; browser still
# revalidates. The catch-all /* gives these only s-maxage=300 (5min CDN), so on
# a low-traffic site edges go cold between visits → repeated origin fetches that
# spiked /crowmark cold LCP to ~3s (warm HIT = 47-73ms). These are referenced
# with ?v= cache-busters, so a longer EDGE TTL is safe (a deploy bumps ?v= → new
# URL → fresh fetch). Browser max-age stays short (300s) to avoid the 4h-stale
# incident class; only the edge/Surrogate TTL is extended.
/js/*
  # `!` UNSETS the inherited value first. Cloudflare Pages CONCATENATES a header
  # when an earlier rule already set it rather than overriding it, so without
  # these the catch-all's values ride along and the response carries two
  # conflicting directives. Measured live 2026-08-01 on a stylesheet:
  #   cdn-cache-control: no-store
  #   cdn-cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  # Both were sent. Which one an edge or a browser honours is undefined.
  ! Cache-Control
  ! CDN-Cache-Control
  ! Surrogate-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, s-maxage=86400, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  Surrogate-Control: max-age=86400
  # Re-asserted because the catch-all now sets `CDN-Cache-Control: no-store`.
  # Safe to keep a long EDGE ttl: these are fetched with ?v= cache-busters, so
  # a deploy changes the URL rather than needing the old one invalidated.
  CDN-Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

